What it says, against README section 2's published row (FFM 33.8 ns, JNI 98.4 ns):

* a plain FFM **downcall** costs 12.5 to 14.7 ns here and a JNI **forward call** 11.4 to
  13.0 on the same JDK, so **FFM is not cheaper than JNI in the forward direction on this
  machine**. The published pair is three times apart and these two are not apart at all.
* `isTrivial()` halves it, to 6.2 to 8.1 ns. A codec entry point that makes reverse calls
  may not declare itself trivial -- that is exactly what the option promises it will not
  do -- so the cheap number is available to the bulk-bytes path of ABI v1 section 8 and to
  nothing else in the codec.
* the published 98.4 ns is an **upcall** figure and the published 11.2 ns a **forward**
  one. This machine's cached JNI upcall is 76 to 91 ns, which is the size class of the
  first; its forward call is 11 to 13, which is the size class of the second. The table
  lists one number per runtime and they are seven times apart.

**What this does NOT say.** It is a preview API on the JDK before the one that finalised
it, measured on a no-op with one long argument. An FFM BINDING is not built here and its
upcall price, which is what the encode path would actually pay, is not measured at all.
